The Human-English states that every continuous lattice is an injective space in its induced topology: if \(X \subseteq Y\) are \(T_0\)-spaces and \(f : X \to D\) is continuous, the formula \(\bar f(y) = \bigsqcup\{\sqcap f(X \cap U) \mid U \text{ open},\ y \in U\}\) defines a continuous extension of \(f\). For continuity, \(d \ll \bar f(y)\) is interpolated to \(d \ll d' \ll \bar f(y)\), the definition places \(d'\) below \(\sqcap f(X \cap U)\) for some open neighbourhood \(U\) of \(y\), and then \(d' \sqsubseteq \bar f(y')\), hence \(d \ll \bar f(y')\), for every \(y' \in U\). For the extension, \(\bar f(x') \sqsubseteq f(x')\) because \(f(x')\) belongs to every set being met, while \(d \ll f(x')\) and continuity of \(f\) produce a neighbourhood on which \(d\) lies below every value, hence below the infimum and below \(\bar f(x')\); continuity of \(D\), writing \(f(x')\) as the supremum of the elements way-below it, promotes that inequality to \(f(x') \sqsubseteq \bar f(x')\). `scottExtend` is that supremum of infima along a comparison \(e\), with \(e^{-1}(V)\) in place of \(X \cap U\). `proposition_2_11` concludes that the Scott topology, which is the induced topology, makes the lattice an `InjectiveSpace`: given an embedding \(e\) and a continuous \(f\), `scottExtend_continuous` and `scottExtend_eq_of_continuous` supply a continuous extension agreeing on the image of \(e\). The nonempty and directed hypotheses needed to take that supremum are `scottExtend_aux_nonempty`, using the whole codomain as a neighbourhood, and `scottExtend_aux_directed`, using that the intersection of two neighbourhoods is a neighbourhood and that shrinking a neighbourhood raises the infimum. The continuity proof does not insert the interpolant \(d \ll d' \ll \bar f(y)\). It takes from the way-below basis an element \(a \ll \bar f(y)\) whose principal upset lies in the given Scott-open set, applies `wayBelow_sSup_iff` to place \(a\) way-below one of the infima, and then uses right monotonicity of \(\ll\) because that same infimum is one term of \(\bar f(y')\) for every \(y'\) in the neighbourhood. `wayBelow_interpolate` is imported and is the device named in the English, but it is not the step written in `scottExtend_continuous`. `scottExtend_eq_of_continuous` follows the two extension inequalities: the original value lies in every image set whose infimum appears, and the reverse pulls the Scott-open set of points way-above an element \(a \ll f(x')\) back along \(f\) and, by the embedding, to an open set of \(Y\). `continuous_eq_sSup_openInfs`, `scottExtend_maximal`, and `scottExtend_maximal_le` add that any Scott-continuous map lying below \(f\) along the embedding lies below `scottExtend`, so the formula is the greatest extension; the proof of 2.11 claims only existence, and these lemmas do not reverse the inequality. **Theorem:** yes — `proposition_2_11` is injectivity under the Scott topology, witnessed by `scottExtend` along an embedding. **Proof:** yes — agreement on the image of the embedding is the two inequalities in the text, and continuity is the same neighbourhood calculation with the way-below basis and clause (vii) in place of a named interpolant.
